-                                <span class="feature-banner-eyebrow">Japan's Weekly Pulse</span>
-                                <h2 class="feature-banner-title">City League Meta — who's winning, who's losing, week over week.</h2>
-                                <p class="feature-banner-tagline">
-                                    Pick <em>City League Meta</em> from the homepage and you land on Japan's full cardshop-circuit snapshot — the largest competitive ecosystem in the world, ~2 300 decks deep, refreshed daily. The page is built around a single question: <em>which deck is winning the format right now, and which way is it moving?</em>
-                                </p>
-                                <ul class="feature-banner-bullets">
-                                    <li><strong>Headline · 2 298 deck entries</strong> — the badge at the top of the page is the sample-size anchor. Every percentage below is computed against this count, so you always know whether you're reading a 7-day burst (small N) or a stabilised 30-day window (large N). Click it to widen the data window.</li>
-                                    <li><strong>Tier brackets with archetype art</strong> — every archetype gets its own card with the actual key-Pokémon TCG art as background and two stat-badges underneath. The orange <code>Rank N</code> badge is the deck's position in the format ranking; the neutral <code>Share X %</code> badge is its slice of total deck entries. Each badge carries an inline <em>▲ / ▼ / —</em> trend chip with the absolute pp / rank change vs the prior identical-length window. <em>Dragapult ex · Rank 1 ▲ 2 · Share 14,2 % ▲ 0,4</em> reads as "moved up 2 ranks and gained 0,4 pp share since last week".</li>
-                                    <li><strong>Tier 1 / 2 / 3 cutoffs are computed, not fixed</strong> — Tier 1 Meta Dominators (red), Tier 2 Strong Contenders (orange), Tier 3 Niche Picks (green). Decks land in a tier based on a rolling composite score: <em>brought share + Top-8 conversion + 30-day WR</em>, not on a hard share threshold. A Tier 3 deck with high Top-8 conversion is your tech-target signal.</li>
-                                    <li><strong>Variant counter ("26 var.")</strong> — the count next to each tier-header tells you how many sub-variants the database recognises for that archetype-family. <em>Dragapult ex · 26 variants</em> means Dragapult-ex, Dragapult Dusknoir, Dragapult Meowth, Dragapult Froslass + 22 more all roll up into that one card. The Full Comparison table below splits them apart if you need per-variant numbers.</li>
-                                    <li><strong>Click any tier card → card-level Deck Analysis</strong> — the entire tier card is a click target. One tap takes you to the archetype's per-card breakdown (Main Cards / Tech / Counter / Rare Picks). Same target also reachable from any of the other tables below.</li>
-                                    <li><strong>Performance Improvers + Decliners panel</strong> (further down the page) — two ranked mini-tables, green "📈 Improvers" + red "📉 Decliners". Each lists the top-5 archetypes whose share % moved by <strong>≥ 0,4 pp</strong> vs the prior window, sorted by absolute Δ. Surfaces "Slowking just jumped +2,3 pp" without scrolling the full comparison table. Decks with insufficient movement are hidden so the panel always shows clean signal.</li>
-                                    <li><strong>Overall vs Top-8 Conversion panel</strong> — ranks every deck two ways at once: <em>Brought Share</em> (raw deck count ÷ total entries) on the left, <em>Top-8 Conversion</em> (Top-8 finishes ÷ same deck's entries) on the right. The "low share, high punch" decks — high Top-8 rank, low Brought-Share rank — are exactly the ones you want to tech against even if they don't dominate the headline. Hydrapple Ogerpon at #1 in Top-8 but outside Top-3 in Brought Share is the canonical example.</li>
-                                    <li><strong>Past Meta toggle</strong> — dropdown at the top swaps the active feed from "Current snapshot" (auto-updating) to a frozen earlier format window (TEF-POR, SVI-ASC, SVI-PFL, …). Same screens, same data shape, different time window — useful for "what was the meta when Charizard ex rotated in?" reads.</li>
-                                </ul>
-                                <div class="feature-banner-protip">
-                                    <strong>Pro tip:</strong> The "<em>Season pause</em>" banner appears between City League seasons when no new events are running — the historical filters and deck-analysis tools below still work against the last available snapshot, so the page never goes dark.
